					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/drnietzsche" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1415487); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1415487&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606208012" alt="DrNietzsche" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1415487)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/drnietzsche" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1415487); return false;" >DrNietzsche</a></span> <span class="odate time_1416798824 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">24 Nov 2014 03:13</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2164831">
									

<p>What's wrong with Humes? The technology isn't all that out-there. I mean, it's extremely over-simplified but there's nothing in real-world science that says you couldn't create a baseline &quot;reality structure&quot; reading.</p>
<p>Let's look at what we <em><strong>know</strong></em> about the Universe. Time is (again, simplifying) the fastest speed of information. What's the fastest way to get information? Light. Therefore, lightspeed is the speed of time. So, we have defined what Time is. From our perspective it's the one-way travel of information.</p>
<p>What can affect time(actually, light)? Gravity. Speed. What, therefore, would be a disruption of reality? A black hole. As far as we know, a black hole breaks a pretty serious law, the law that information <em><strong>cannot</strong></em> be destroyed. If this law is indeed a constant, then a black hole is a breakdown of the structure of reality, that is (apparently, thankfully) self-contained. It can't expand because it's pulling at itself just as strong as it's pulling at everything else.</p>
<p>Using this as a base, we can therefore make assumptions on what <em>else</em> might affect local reality. Like, say, super-high distortions of gravity that do not exist long enough to affect anything but the largest fundamental forces. It doesn't rip us apart(normally) because it's only around long enough to exert an influence on light(time) and what have you.</p>
<p>How would you read this? Well, if we're assuming this is working using super-powerful gravity, you just get a super-sensitive measure and tell it to record *every* change no matter how minute, and to alert you if it goes beyond what you'd consider the normal fluctuation of gravity on earth(which is a thing).</p>
<p>There are other ways you could probably bend reality over a table and fuck it, probably through super-strong magnetic forces or the strong nuclear-forces, but gravity is the one we know for certain that works, and it's one we can reliably test and measure.</p>
<p>Now, this is all pretty theoretical, but it is based in hard science. I'm making assumptions on the nature of black holes and how time actually works, but that's only because that's all we have right now. I also probably have numerous errors in this, which someone will hopefully point out.</p>

